<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saigo Takamori</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1828">1828</a>–<a href="/years/1877">1877</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Saigō Takamori</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saigo Takamori was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-domain">Satsuma Domain</a> and one of the leaders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a> of 1868. He commanded imperial forces in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boshin-war">Boshin War</a>, negotiated the peaceful surrender of Edo and arranged lenient terms for defeated domains such as Shonai. Opposed to the new government's policies, he led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-rebellion">Satsuma Rebellion</a> of 1877 and died when it was crushed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saigo-takamori.jpg" alt="Saigo Takamori" width="320" height="431" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: C. Nakagawa (from signature) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saigo_Takamori_(b).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
